      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The authors measured as a routine IgA, IgG and IgM serum immunoglobulins in a hundred subjects free from any cardiac or hypertensive disease, in 51 patients with coronary heart disease in in 50 hypertensive subjects. They found that in 2% of the normal subjects and in 28% of the coronary heart disease or hypertension, the level of one of the three serum immunoglobulins was outside the following limits: IgA 100 to 480 mg/100 ml, IgG 500 to 1900 mg/100 ml and IgM 45 to 380 mg/100 ml. The 2 normal subjects and 12% of the patients with coronary heart disease or hypertension presented an abnormally low level of one of the immunoglobulins, while an abnormally high level was observed in 16% of the subjects with coronary heart disease or hypertension. The abnormal serum levels may concern any class of immunoglobulin and persist in the case of some patients with coronary heart disease several years after the acute ischemic incident."},
